===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}} {{term-label|en|linguistics}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[study#Noun|study]] of the [[way#Noun|way]] [[sound#Noun|sounds]] [[function#Verb|function]] in [[language]]s, including [[accent#Noun|accent]], [[intonation]], [[phoneme]]s, [[stress#Noun|stress]], and [[syllable#Noun|syllable]] [[structure#Noun|structure]], and which sounds are [[distinctive#Adjective|distinctive]] [[unit#Noun|units]] within a language; {{lb|en|countable}} the way sounds function within a [[given#Adjective|given]] language; a [[phonological]] [[system]].
#: {{synonyms|en|orthoepy|soundlore<q:dated>}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1856|title=Journal of the Indian Archipelago and Eastern Asia|publisher=Mission Press|page=16|passage=The Achean, the ancient Malayu and other mixed '''phonologies''' possessing a considerable degree of harshness, were thus formed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=Jacek Fisiak|title=Trends in Linguistics: Studies in Middle English Linguistics|isbn=3110152428|publisher=Walter de Gruyter|page=545
|passage=Crucially, the neat separateness of '''phonologies''' which my account seems to imply is an abstraction and does not mean that the '''phonologies''' represented different regional or social dialects.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=Charles W. Kreidler|title=Phonology|page=219
|passage=Thus, underlying ‘agtus’ was converted first into ‘āgtus’ by the vowel lengthening rule, and then into ‘āktus’ by the ancient persistent rule. This example has previously been interpreted as indicating that new rules can enter a '''phonology''' elsewhere than at depth I.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension|uncountable}} The study of the way [[component#Noun|components]] of [[sign#Noun|signs]] function in a [[sign language]], and which components are distinctive units within the language; {{lb|en|countable}} the way components of signs function within a given sign language.

